## 1. Methodology for Index Calculation

### 1.1 Baker, Bloom & Davis (2016) – Economic Policy Uncertainty (EPU)

- **Data Collection:**  
  - Utilized a panel of 10 major U.S. newspapers (e.g. *New York Times*, *Wall Street Journal*, *Washington Post*, among others) starting from 1985.  
  - A historical extension back to 1900 was created using a subset of long-running papers.  
  - Articles were retrieved from digital archives (e.g., Newsbank, ProQuest) on a monthly basis.

- **Keyword/Dictionary Approach:**  
  - Articles were classified as policy-uncertainty related only if they contained a *triple of terms*: one word related to the **economy** (e.g. “economic” or “economy”), one word for **uncertainty** (e.g. “uncertain” or “uncertainty”), and at least one **policy-related term** (e.g. “Congress”, “regulation”, “White House”).  
  - This conservative filter was chosen to ensure that only articles directly addressing policy uncertainty were counted.  
  - Adjustments were made for historical contexts (e.g. additional terms relevant to earlier periods).

- **Index Construction:**  
  - For each newspaper and month, the count of qualifying articles was normalized by total news volume.  
  - The normalized series was then averaged across newspapers and scaled so that the index has a base value of 100 over a standard sample period (e.g. 1985–2009).  
  - The resulting EPU index reliably spikes around major economic and policy events.

